18	The Norman Conquest of England in 1066 by William I was one of the greatest conquests in history, with the Anglo-Saxon defeat at Hastings, and William crowned by Christmas 1066 it was very quick but effective. This essay will delve into the consequences of the invasion.\r\n\r\nFirst of all, the most immediate consequence was the fact that England had now moved from an Anglo-Saxon monarchy into a Norman monarchy, and that in itself was the domain of larger change. For example, all lords who controlled the lands were Anglo-Saxon previously, but now a minority of lords were Anglo-Saxon with most of them being castle-building Norman lords who William I had awarded land to due to their service in helping him conquer England. While the villeins and serfs weren't affected that much by the new rulers, as all that changed for them was having a new lord, it still affected the cultural landscape due to the fact that the Norman lords spoke French while the lower class Anglo-Saxon peasants spoke Old English, and language was a difficult barrier.\r\n\r\nThe long-term consequences were much more drawn out, across centuries even. Socially, Norman lords built castles to show their superiority over the Anglo-Saxon villeins and this created a large tension between the two separate communities. Politically, there were even more laws created. While the Anglo-Saxon law system was kept, the Normans added trial by combat, Murdrum fines, Royal Hunting laws, and many more. Culturally, there were new words added to the English language, mostly those of posh style such as pork - Normans would have pork, English would have pig. \r\n\r\nOverall, the Norman Conquest was influential in establishing new words, creating new laws, castles, and community-division. 	24	\\xd5a83f1806724e22b374c19ede76b44c87fb677ae67f42df92f5fb657a74c99d	2025-02-23 15:35:24.969	B	Finished	{"point": "The main point of your essay is clear: the Norman Conquest transformed England's monarchy and had significant social, political, and cultural consequences. This is well-articulated in your introduction and throughout the body.", "evidence": "You provide solid evidence to support your claims, such as the shift in land ownership from Anglo-Saxon lords to Norman lords and the introduction of new laws. The examples of language differences, like 'pork' versus 'pig', effectively illustrate cultural changes.", "analysis": "Your analysis of the immediate and long-term consequences is insightful. You highlight how these changes affected societal structures and introduce the idea of community tension, which invites further exploration of these dynamics."}	"While your essay is informative, it could benefit from a clearer structure. Consider separating immediate and long-term consequences into distinct sections for better organization. Additionally, providing more specific examples of cultural changes beyond language would strengthen your argument."	{"structure_and_sequencing": 15, "person_and_tense": 15, "sentence_structure": 16, "language_and_vocabulary": 18, "purpose_style_and_tone": 15, "sense_and_accuracy": 10}	11
22	The Indian Army is one of the most powerful and respected military forces in the world, known for its bravery, discipline, and dedication to protecting the nation. Established on April 1, 1895, it plays a crucial role in safeguarding India's borders, maintaining peace, and assisting in disaster relief operations. The army has been instrumental in various wars, including the Indo-Pak wars and the Kargil conflict, showcasing immense courage and strategic excellence. With a strong motto of "Service Before Self," the Indian Army not only defends the country but also contributes to internal security and humanitarian missions. It consists of different regiments and divisions, each specializing in various forms of combat, from infantry and artillery to armored corps and special forces. The soldiers undergo rigorous training to enhance their skills, ensuring they remain prepared for any challenge. The army also participates in international peacekeeping missions, reflecting India's commitment to global peace. The sacrifices made by Indian soldiers, who put their lives on the line for the nation's safety, are a testament to their unwavering patriotism. Their dedication and valor inspire millions, making the Indian Army a symbol of national pride and honor.	36	\\x0f808708e9534cf5baf0c4f843bf02ee1bc1bd93e2cc40dba96d256218a305bb	2025-02-24 08:42:10.46	B	Finished	{"Point": "The main point of the work is clear: the Indian Army is portrayed as a powerful and respected military force, dedicated to national and global peace. This thesis is supported effectively throughout the text by highlighting its history, roles, and values.", "Evidence": "The evidence provided is both factual and relevant, mentioning specific historical events like the Indo-Pak wars and the Kargil conflict, which strengthen the argument. The inclusion of the army's motto and its participation in international peacekeeping missions adds credibility to the claims made.", "Analysis": "The analysis effectively interprets the evidence, emphasizing the army's dual role in defense and humanitarian efforts. The mention of the soldiers' sacrifices and the inspiration they provide to the nation adds depth to the narrative."}	"While the content is strong, the essay would benefit from more structured paragraphs to improve flow. Adding subheadings could help organize different aspects of the Indian Army\\u2019s roles and contributions. Additionally, incorporating more vivid descriptions and examples could enhance engagement and emotional impact."	{"structure_and_sequencing": 15, "person_and_tense": 15, "sentence_structure": 16, "language_and_vocabulary": 18, "purpose_style_and_tone": 15, "sense_and_accuracy": 10}	15
