1581	\\xdd6fe71f4006445a86c5822832ccd29f3ab187d7b86b4b5bb43eaf9284745ef8	528	\N	B	A	In an AC circuit, the current through a capacitor leads the voltage across it by 90 degrees. This is because the capacitor stores energy in the form of an electric field, and the current must flow to charge the capacitor before the voltage can build up. This 90-degree phase difference is a fundamental characteristic of capacitive elements in AC circuits.	3	2025-03-25 13:12:43.351	49496745-ec9d-4646-9a77-e89e8f205407	23	209
1582	\\xcdd1c556d3224e44a4ee01e86f579588d16f489a34024f3fb9438b0db69636f1	529	\N	B	D	In an AC circuit, the voltage across an inductor leads the current through it by 90 degrees. This is because the inductor opposes changes in current, causing the current to lag behind the voltage. The phase difference is a result of the inductive reactance, which is given by XL = ωL, where ω is the angular frequency and L is the inductance.	1	2025-03-25 13:12:43.361	49496745-ec9d-4646-9a77-e89e8f205407	23	209
1583	\\x9966cf4dda7c4869a7571e0874c2ee1d1bcc9079ecdf4673a9c18270a1f2c1e8	530	\N	A	C	The capacitive reactance (XC) in an AC circuit is given by the formula XC = 1/(ωC), where ω is the angular frequency (ω = 2πf) and C is the capacitance. Since ω is directly proportional to the frequency (f), the capacitive reactance is inversely proportional to the frequency. This means that as the frequency increases, the capacitive reactance decreases, and vice versa.	3	2025-03-25 13:12:43.369	49496745-ec9d-4646-9a77-e89e8f205407	23	209
1584	\\xeded8e7043554e7792973f2e7d373d977da2ca3041234c23aef200a3ce779548	531	\N	B	C	In a purely capacitive AC circuit, the current leads the voltage by 90 degrees. This is because the capacitor stores energy in the form of an electric field, and the current must flow to charge the capacitor before the voltage can build up. This 90-degree phase difference is a fundamental characteristic of capacitive elements in AC circuits.	2	2025-03-25 13:12:43.377	49496745-ec9d-4646-9a77-e89e8f205407	23	209
1585	\\xe599f94c85384e59b2e6eaf3554a5da5f5c731cd05554b4c929c7ee04697d14e	532	\N	B	D	In a purely inductive AC circuit, the current lags the voltage by 90 degrees. This is because the inductor stores energy in the form of a magnetic field, and the voltage must build up before the current can flow. This 90-degree phase difference is a fundamental characteristic of inductive elements in AC circuits.	2	2025-03-25 13:12:43.384	49496745-ec9d-4646-9a77-e89e8f205407	23	209
1586	\\x0db0e4236ea0426b81a8798483c9cce9cba7cc24b35d428887c2a2210bb60bfa	533	\N	A	C	The power factor is defined as the cosine of the phase angle between voltage and current. In a purely resistive circuit, the voltage and current are in phase, making the phase angle 0 degrees. The cosine of 0 degrees is 1, so the power factor is 1. This indicates that all the power is real power and is being effectively used in the circuit.	3	2025-03-25 13:12:43.392	49496745-ec9d-4646-9a77-e89e8f205407	23	209
1587	\\xb4b151221e9c428a91a8553620efbb05fcfed03c72484b508c203715082197de	534	\N	A	D	Reactance, whether inductive (XL) or capacitive (XC), is a measure of the opposition that inductors or capacitors offer to the flow of alternating current. Like resistance, reactance is measured in ohms (Ω). Inductive reactance is given by XL = 2πfL, and capacitive reactance is given by XC = 1/(2πfC), where f is the frequency, L is the inductance, and C is the capacitance.	3	2025-03-25 13:12:43.401	49496745-ec9d-4646-9a77-e89e8f205407	23	209
